&lt;p&gt;The jokes come from real news. Every line is written or rewritten by me. The models are characters here, not writers: LLM jokes are polite, and satire can't be.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The pipeline
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is no video generation anywhere in it. Everything is code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Script.&lt;/strong&gt; I start from a news story and an LLM draft through OpenRouter (about $0.04 per draft), then rewrite most of it by hand. An episode is 83 lines, about 4 minutes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Voices.&lt;/strong&gt; Each character has its own ElevenLabs voice. Episode 3 in English is 4,308 characters of speech, and lines are cached, so a rewrite only pays for the lines that changed. Laughs are voiced too: a cartoon that laughs in silence looks broken.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Animation.&lt;/strong&gt; The characters are layered PNG puppets in &lt;a href="https://www.remotion.dev/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Remotion&lt;/a&gt;, so the whole episode is a React component. Mouths move from word timestamps, eyes blink, hands gesture while the character talks. Each character has a set of emotions and full-body poses for reactions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F0v9gy0r6vzt4tu5lcwum.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F0v9gy0r6vzt4tu5lcwum.jpg" alt="One character, five states: happy, angry, shocked, facepalm, laughing" width="800" height="160"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Direction.&lt;/strong&gt; A small JSON file per episode says who reacts to whom and when: a facepalm after a line, a laugh during someone else's punchline, a record scratch when the host panics. Sound effects are generated once and reused.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Captions.&lt;/strong&gt; Burned in, the current word highlighted in the speaker's colour. Swear words are audible but always shown as #@!.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F55rx47lqoptd5334tjim.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F55rx47lqoptd5334tjim.jpg" alt="Captions and the speaker's colour" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Checks.&lt;/strong&gt; Whisper listens to every take and flags wrong words. I check stress and timing by ear.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Render.&lt;/strong&gt; On my own server, not my laptop. A 4-minute 1080p episode takes about 70 minutes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What broke
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Characters reacted before they spoke.&lt;/strong&gt; A reaction pose over a character's own voice looked like they answered a line nobody had said yet. Fix: whoever speaks is always the lip-synced puppet, and reaction poses are only for listeners.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Stress marks broke a word.&lt;/strong&gt; The show is made in Russian first. A stress mark meant to fix pronunciation turned one word into a different, ruder word. Whisper caught it before I did.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;My first $10 ad went to Cyprus.&lt;/strong&gt; 70% of the paid views came from one small country. The fix is one setting: target people who are in the location, not people "interested" in it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

