&lt;h1&gt;


  ¿que son los hooks en react?
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Los Hooks son una característica introducida en React 16.8 que permiten a los componentes funcionales usar estado, ciclo de vida y otras funcionalidades de React sin necesidad de convertirlos en componentes de clase.Antes de los Hooks, solo los componentes de clase podían manejar estado, "this.state" o usar métodos del ciclo de vida, como "componentDidMount", "componentDidUpdate", etc. Con los Hooks, los componentes funcionales se volvieron mucho más potentes y se convirtieron en la forma recomendada de escribir componentes en React. ¿Para qué sirven? Los Hooks permiten: Manejar estado local Ejecutar efectos secundarios, llamadas a APIs, suscripciones, manipulación del DOM, etc.. Compartir lógica reutilizable entre componentes. Optimizar el rendimiento. Acceder al contexto, referencias y otras características de React. Reglas de los Hooks. React impone dos reglas importantes: Solo se pueden llamar en el nivel superior: No se deben usar dentro de condicionales (if), bucles o funciones anidadas. Solo se pueden llamar desde componentes funcionales de React o desde Hooks personalizados: No se deben usar en funciones JavaScript normales. Estas reglas permiten a React mantener el estado de los Hooks de forma consistente entre renderizados. Hooks más utilizados useState – Manejo de estado import { useState } from 'react'; export default function Contador() { const [contador, setContador] = useState(0); return ( &lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Contador: {contador}&lt;/p&gt;  setContador(contador + 1)}&amp;gt; Incrementar   ); } useEffect – Efectos secundarios Se usa para ejecutar código después del renderizado, equivalente a "componentDidMount", "componentDidUpdate" y "componentWillUnmount". import { useState, useEffect } from 'react'; export default function EjemploEfecto() { const [datos, setDatos] = useState(null); useEffect(() =&amp;gt; { // Se ejecuta después del renderizado fetch('&lt;a href="https://api.ejemplo.com/datos'" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://api.ejemplo.com/datos'&lt;/a&gt;) .then(res =&amp;gt; res.json()) .then(data =&amp;gt; setDatos(data)); // Función de limpieza (opcional) return () =&amp;gt; { console.log('Limpiando...'); }; }, []); // Array de dependencias vacío = solo se ejecuta al montar return {datos ? JSON.stringify(datos) : 'Cargando...'}; } useContext – Consumir contexto import { useContext } from 'react'; import { TemaContext } from './TemaContext'; export default function Boton() { const tema = useContext(TemaContext); return Botón; } Otros Hooks comunes "useRef": Referencias a elementos del DOM o valores mutables. "useMemo": Memorizar valores calculados. "useCallback": Memorizar funciones. "useReducer": Manejo de estado más complejo. "useLayoutEffect": Similar a "useEffect, pero se ejecuta de forma síncrona después del DOM. Hooks personalizados También se pueden crear Hooks personalizados para reutilizar lógica. Deben empezar con la palabra use: import { useState, useEffect } from 'react'; function useTitulo(titulo) { useEffect(() =&amp;gt; { document.title = titulo; }, [titulo]); } export default function Pagina() { useTitulo('Mi página'); return &lt;h1&gt;Contenido&lt;/h1&gt;; } Resumen Los Hooks permiten usar características de React en componentes funcionales. Hacen el código más legible y fácil de reutilizar. Deben seguir las reglas de los Hooks. Los más importantes son "useState" y "useEffect".

&lt;p&gt;Streaming text can reassure a sighted user that a feature is working. The same stream can become exhausting or impossible to follow when every fragment is announced as a new message, controls disappear, or focus moves while the person is reading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The interface needs to communicate two things separately: the operation’s status and the generated content. Someone should be able to learn that generation started, stop it, and choose when to inspect the result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This React example uses a stable status region, an ordinary result container, and explicit controls. A recorded text fixture makes cancellation, focus, and completion repeatable without model credentials. Native model quality is outside this component’s responsibility.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Give status its own region
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The status element exists before generation starts. It announces meaningful transitions rather than individual tokens.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;role&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;aria&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;live&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"polite"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;aria&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;atomic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"true"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;ref&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resultRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;tabIndex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;aria&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Generated response"&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;aria&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;busy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;busy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The result container has no live-region attribute. The user can read it through normal navigation or move focus there using an explicit review action.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a design choice to test with assistive technology, not a promise that all browser and screen-reader combinations behave identically. W3C’s status-message guidance focuses on making relevant changes available without unnecessarily taking focus. It does not prescribe announcing an entire evolving answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Define the transitions before connecting the model
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The component has four meaningful outcomes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Event&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Status&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Result handling&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Start&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Generation is in progress&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Clear the previous result&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Complete&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Response is ready for review&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Enable explicit review&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stop&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Generation stopped&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Mark partial content incomplete&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Failure&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Generation failed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Preserve any partial text with its limitation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Avoid putting every network event into the status region. A person does not need to hear each retry, token, or internal progress callback to understand whether the feature is working.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Downloads may deserve their own progress interaction, especially if they take time. Keep that distinct from answer generation and avoid repeatedly announcing tiny percentage changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhow41vluht8yt6s3p7rf.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhow41vluht8yt6s3p7rf.png" alt="Start branches to completion, cancellation, or failure; completion enables explicit review while focus stays with the user's action." width="800" height="529"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Figure 1. Announce operation states and let the person decide when to enter the result.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Make cancellation an application state
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The stream adapter receives an &lt;code&gt;AbortSignal&lt;/code&gt;. The component also checks request identity before appending text, because a cancelled or superseded source can still produce a late callback.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;delta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;buffer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;delta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;buffer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This adapter contract says chunks are deltas. If the provider returns cumulative snapshots, normalize them before passing them to the component. Appending snapshots as deltas would repeat the response and create a different accessibility problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Stop action invalidates the request, aborts its signal, and updates the status. It does not automatically move focus into the partial output. It also does not label that output as ready to use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If generation has already triggered a remote side effect, stopping this stream cannot undo that action. The example streams text only, which keeps the cancellation promise understandable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Keep the controls stable
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Native buttons provide keyboard activation and recognizable semantics. Avoid replacing the Generate button with a differently positioned Stop button during the request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The example keeps both controls mounted. It uses &lt;code&gt;aria-disabled&lt;/code&gt; for temporary unavailability and guards the handler, so an attribute alone is never relied on to prevent an operation. This also avoids removing the focused Stop button immediately after cancellation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Review button becomes available only after completion:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;disabled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;complete&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resultRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;focus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Review&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Focus moves because the user requested it. Completion alone does not pull the person away from another field they are editing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep visible focus styles, readable contrast, and sensible zoom behavior. A correct ARIA attribute cannot compensate for a control that disappears off screen or becomes impossible to identify.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Test behavior before testing the generated wording
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The companion includes a recorded stream that emits a provisional room-hold message in small chunks. It is explicitly labeled as a fixture. This lets the test stop at a known point, inspect focus, and confirm that later chunks do not appear.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The browser checks passed in Headless Chrome 153: cancellation prevented later fixture output, explicit review moved focus, and streamed content stayed outside the live status region. The 390-pixel layout had no horizontal overflow. An axe scan found zero violations in the tested completed state, with 36 checks passing. These observations are recorded in the companion evidence file; they are not a substitute for listening with a screen reader.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The deterministic request-lifecycle test is executed separately and verifies that a superseded request cannot remain current. That establishes a useful component invariant without making an accessibility-conformance claim.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Listen to the experience
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Complete a manual session with at least one actual browser and screen-reader pairing. Record the versions and the task rather than writing “screen readers supported.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start generation from the keyboard. Move to another control while output arrives. Confirm that the status is understandable and that focus stays where intended. Cancel, restart, finish, and use Review response. Test a provider error and a long answer as separate cases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also inspect what happens when someone reads the evolving result directly. Suppressing automatic announcements should not prevent voluntary access to the content. Different users may prefer a separate option to announce a completed answer; evaluate that choice rather than assuming one setting serves everyone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Automated checks and DOM assertions can catch structural mistakes. They cannot establish whether announcements are timely, understandable, or exhausting. This draft makes no claim of a completed VoiceOver or NVDA listening session.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A streaming interface succeeds when people can understand and control the operation while continuing their work. What does your AI feature announce today, and have you listened to the full interaction from start to failure as well as start to success?&lt;/p&gt;

